# Decode Lab P3

This project is a Node.js/Express backend for authentication and study log management. It connects to MongoDB, signs JWT tokens, and sends OTP emails using Gmail.

## Environment Setup

Create a `.env` file in the project root directory and add the following values:

```env
MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://<username>:<password>@<cluster-url>/<database-name>
JWT_SECRET=your_super_secret_key
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=30d
GMAIL_USER=your_email@gmail.com
GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=your_16_character_gmail_app_password
PORT=6000
```

### Required variables
- `MONGODB_URI`: MongoDB connection string.
- `JWT_SECRET`: Secret key used to sign and verify authentication tokens.
- `JWT_EXPIRES_IN`: JWT expiration time. Optional, but the app already defaults to `30d`.
- `GMAIL_USER`: Gmail account used to send OTP emails.
- `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD`: Gmail app password generated from Google account settings.
- `PORT`: Port used by the app. The app currently defaults to `6000`.

> Important: the app currently uses `const port = 6000 || process.env.PORT;` in `index.js`, which means `6000` will usually be used even when `PORT` is set. If you want the environment variable to control the port, update it to:
>
> ```js
> const port = process.env.PORT || 6000;
> ```

## Project Path and Folder Requirements

This project must be run from the root folder where the main files are located.

Example structure:

```text
Decode Lab P3/
├── index.js
├── auth.js
├── models.js
├── utils.js
├── package.json
├── .env
├── node_modules/
└── README
```

The app uses relative imports like:

```js
require('./auth')
require('./models')
require('./utils')
```

These paths only work correctly if:
- all files stay in the same project folder,
- you run the app from the project root,
- the `.env` file is also located in that same project root folder.

If the files are moved or run from a different directory, the imports and environment config may fail.

## Install Dependencies

From the project root, run:

```bash
npm install
```

## Start the Application

```bash
node index.js
```

or

```bash
nodemon index.js
```

## Notes
- Make sure MongoDB is running and your connection string is valid.
- Ensure your Gmail account has app passwords enabled.
- Keep your `.env` file private and do not commit it to GitHub.
- If you are using a production or shared environment, set the environment variables in your hosting platform instead of storing them in a local file.

